ZSignal<Window*> UIZSignals::ThemeReapply;   // re-apply to visible subtree after page/tab switch Color priority: explicit instance color > window theme > app theme . Once a control's Set*Color is called explicitly it is flagged "user-colored" and the theme no longer overrides it ; all other controls follow the theme. Pitfall Backdrop is the window/background base color (white in light, black in dark); after an explicit SetBackdrop/SetBackgroundColor tint, the theme no longer changes it. Version : introduced in v1.19.0 ; window periphery (caption buttons) follows the system theme, not the window theme; WM_SETTINGCHANGE/WM_THEMECHANGED/WM_DWMCOLORIZATIONCOLORCHANGED/WM_SYSCOLORCHANGE auto re-resolve.

cpp Copy #include "ZufyUI.h" #include "ZufyUIWidgets.h" #include "ZufyUIDsl.h" using namespace ZufyUI ; using namespace ZufyUI :: dsl ; int APIENTRY wWinMain ( HINSTANCE , HINSTANCE , LPWSTR , int ) { auto nameBox = ZTxt (). placeholder ( L "Type here..." ); auto greeting = ZLbl ( L "Hello, waiting..." ); auto greetBtn = ZBtn ( L "Greet" ); auto root = ZCol ( ZRow ( ZLbl ( L "Your name:" ), nameBox ). spacing ( 12 ), greeting . vWeight ( 1 ), ZRow ( greetBtn ). spacing ( 12 ) ). spacing ( 10 ). margin ( Thickness ( 20 )); greetBtn . on <& Button :: Clicked > ([ nameBox , greeting ] { auto name = nameBox -> GetText (); greeting -> SetText ( name . empty () ? L "Hello, stranger." : L "Hello, " + name + L "!" ); }); return ZApp (). title ( L "ZufyUI DSL example" ). size ( 760 , 520 ). root ( root ). run (); } #include "ZufyUI.h"
#include "ZufyUIWidgets.h"
#include "ZufyUIDsl.h"

using namespace ZufyUI;
using namespace ZufyUI::dsl;

int APIENTRY wWinMain(HINSTANCE, HINSTANCE, LPWSTR, int) {
    auto nameBox = ZTxt().placeholder(L"Type here...");
    auto greeting = ZLbl(L"Hello, waiting...");
    auto greetBtn = ZBtn(L"Greet");

    auto root = ZCol(
        ZRow(ZLbl(L"Your name:"), nameBox).spacing(12),
        greeting.vWeight(1),
        ZRow(greetBtn).spacing(12)
    ).spacing(10).margin(Thickness(20));

    greetBtn.on<&Button::Clicked>([nameBox, greeting] {
        auto name = nameBox->GetText();
        greeting->SetText(name.empty() ? L"Hello, stranger." : L"Hello, " + name + L"!");
    });

    return ZApp().title(L"ZufyUI DSL example").size(760, 520).root(root).run();
}  ZTxt() / ZLbl() / ZBtn() return handles to already-created controls ; nameBox is not an empty shell. You do not need to write Ref<TextBox>() first and wait for it to become a control. ZApp().run() already does Create → apply options → Show → Run ; do not call win->Show() again. If the project uses a precompiled header, put #include "pch.h" first; that is project configuration, not a ZufyUI API.  Recommended Inline when possible: ZRow(ZLbl(L"Name"), ZTxt().placeholder(L"...")) . Keep auto nameBox = ZTxt() only when you need to read or modify that control later; do not use an empty Ref<T>() as a placeholder.

auto nameBox = ZTxt(); returns Ref<TextBox> holding a real std::shared_ptr<TextBox> ; auto hides the type, and normal use never needs to spell Ref . Ref<T>() is an empty handle , not a factory: it creates no control, ZRow / ZCol ignore it, and dereferencing it gives a null pointer. Normal UI code does not need it. Only use a default-constructed Ref for a class member assigned later or for an explicitly nullable reference; even then std::shared_ptr<T> with a member initializer is usually clearer. If you do not want the Ref fluent sugar, DSL containers also accept shared_ptr ; use the core Connect for events:  cpp Copy auto nameBox = std :: make_shared < TextBox > (); nameBox -> SetPlaceholder ( L "Type here..." ); auto root = ZCol ( ZRow ( ZLbl ( L "Your name:" ), nameBox ). spacing ( 12 ) ); nameBox -> Connect ( nameBox -> TextChanged , []( const std :: wstring & ) {}); auto nameBox = std::make_shared<TextBox>();
nameBox->SetPlaceholder(L"Type here...");

auto root = ZCol(
    ZRow(ZLbl(L"Your name:"), nameBox).spacing(12)
);

nameBox->Connect(nameBox->TextChanged, [](const std::wstring&) {});  Class-member example (do not treat an empty Ref as ordinary construction):  cpp Copy dsl :: Ref < Label > status_ ; // empty handle; wait for assignment status_ = dsl :: ZLbl ( L "Ready" ); // now points at a real control dsl::Ref<Label> status_;          // empty handle; wait for assignment
status_ = dsl::ZLbl(L"Ready");    // now points at a real control

Chain directly into .run() :  cpp Copy return ZApp () . title ( L "ZufyUI DSL example" ) . size ( 760 , 520 ) . root ( ZCol ( ZLbl ( L "Hello" ))) . run (); return ZApp()
    .title(L"ZufyUI DSL example")
    .size(760, 520)
    .root(ZCol(ZLbl(L"Hello")))
    .run();  To keep the builder, construct it first and call methods as statements:  cpp Copy WindowApp app = ZApp (); app . title ( L "ZufyUI DSL example" ); app . size ( 760 , 520 ); app . root ( ZCol ( ZLbl ( L "Hello" ))); return app . run (); WindowApp app = ZApp();
app.title(L"ZufyUI DSL example");
app.size(760, 520);
app.root(ZCol(ZLbl(L"Hello")));
return app.run();  Do not write auto app = ZApp().title(...) : chain methods return WindowApp& , and WindowApp has a deleted copy constructor, so that fails to compile. WindowApp is movable but not copyable; chain directly into .run() , or start with WindowApp app = ZApp(); .

